  {
   "name": "Darren Waller",
   "position": "TE",
   "nfl_team": "CAR",
   "proj": 8.61,
   "ros_rank": 138.0,
   "fp_waiver_rank": 15.0,
   "injury": "ACTIVE",
   "replaces": "Mason Taylor",
   "ros_rank_gain": 7.0,
   "week_proj_gain": -0.2,
   "priority": "LOW",
   "availability": "ESPN_WAIVERS",
   "espn_pct_owned": 7.7,
   "fp_waiver_note": "With the Panthers' wide receivers having trouble shaking free from the coverage of the Broncos' cornerbacks in Week 9, Darren Waller stepped up and had his biggest game of the season, with 8-90-1 on a team-high 10 targets. Waller ran routes on 69% of QB Bryce Young's dropbacks.\n\nSeemingly bothered by a toe injury, Waller had only two catches in his previous three games &mdash; although one of them went for a 50-yard touchdown. We might have thrown Waller into the Fool's Gold section of this article if not for a favorable Week 10 matchup with the Saints.\n\nEntering Week 9, the Saints had allowed the seventh-most fantasy points per game to tight ends. New Orleans has allowed 47-521-5 to TEs in eight games. The Saints had kept Cleveland's Harold Fannin Jr. under wraps for most of their game against the Browns last week until Fannin bounced off some inept Saints tackle attempts to score a late 39-yard touchdown.\n\nWaller is a viable Week 10 streaming option."
  },
  {
   "name": "Tyler Higbee",
   "position": "TE",
   "nfl_team": "LAR",
   "proj": 7.24,
   "ros_rank": 127.0,
   "fp_waiver_rank": 18.0,
   "injury": "ACTIVE",
   "replaces": "Mason Taylor",
   "ros_rank_gain": 18.0,
   "week_proj_gain": -1.57,
   "priority": "LOW",
   "availability": "ESPN_WAIVERS",
   "espn_pct_owned": 10.0,
   "fp_waiver_note": "Tyler Higbee had 18-212-1 over a three-game stretch from Week 3 to Week 5. Since then, he's had 22 or fewer receiving yards in four consecutive games.\n\nDespite the downturn, this might be a good time to buy in on Higbee. WR Davante Adams is expected to be out for at least another 5-6 weeks with a severe hamstring injury. WR Puka Nacua sustained a knee injury of unknown severity on Sunday. TE Terrance Ferguson was on his way back from an ankle injury when he picked up a hamstring injury in practice last week.\n\nHead coach Sean McVay has leaned aggressively into multiple-TE sets the last two seasons and may have to do so out of necessity in the weeks to come.\n\nHigbee led the Rams' tight ends in snaps (60), routes (35) and targets (5) in Sunday's 20-13 win over the Commanders, although his five targets netted only a single 13-yard catch.\n\nStill, Higbee has a promising target outlook this week against an Arizona defense that has allowed the eighth-most fantasy points to tight ends."
  },
